---
import Base                    from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav                     from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import FeatureBlock            from '../../components/FeatureBlock.astro';
import Footer                  from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import SectionEyebrow          from '../../components/SectionEyebrow.astro';
import KnotArchitectureDiagram from '../../components/KnotArchitectureDiagram.astro';
import '../../styles/global.css';

// LEAN REWRITE 2026-07-28 (owner decision: "too much content is a dead end").
// The page was ~10 sections; a fresh-eyes review (recruiter / developer /
// skimmer) all bailed before the download. New job of this page: say what it
// is, show it, say what it does, and get you to the download fast. Deep
// material (full architecture spec, mesh diagram, benchmark methodology, long
// roadmap, 7-question FAQ) is deliberately CUT from the page and will live in
// the forthcoming Knot white paper. Do NOT restore the removed sections here.
---

<Base
  title="Knot · Peer-to-peer git that commits when you save"
  description="Knot turns any folder into a git repository that commits when you save and syncs to your teammates' machines over Tor. For macOS and Linux."
>
  <Nav />

  <!-- ── HERO ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="relative pt-32 pb-16 px-6 overflow-hidden">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-0 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[80%] h-[28rem] bg-accent/[0.08] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-1/3 right-0 w-72 h-72 bg-blue-500/[0.04] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>

    <div class="relative max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-accent/30 bg-accent/5 text-accent text-xs font-mono tracking-wider mb-8">
        <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-accent animate-pulse"></span>
        KNOT · MACOS + LINUX
      </div>

      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-black tracking-tight leading-[1.05] mb-6">
        <span class="text-primary">Your code.</span><br/>
        <span class="text-gradient">Your machines.</span><br/>
        <span class="text-primary">No middleman.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="text-lg text-secondary font-light max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Knot watches the folders you pick. You hit save, and ten seconds of quiet later there is a real commit, on its way to every machine you paired with.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-accent font-mono mb-10 max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        If pushing to GitHub already works for you, you probably do not need this.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4">
        <a
          href="#download"
          class="px-8 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-accent text-bg font-bold text-sm hover:bg-white transition-all hover:scale-105 hover:shadow-[0_0_32px_rgba(0,212,170,0.4)]"
        >
          Download for Mac / Linux
        </a>
        <a
          href="#how"
          class="px-8 py-3.5 rounded-xl border border-border text-secondary text-sm hover:border-accent hover:text-primary transition-all"
        >
          How it works
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── PRODUCT SHOT (single static image, was a 6-slide carousel) ────────── -->
  <section class="relative pb-24 px-6 overflow-hidden">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-1/4 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[70%] h-72 bg-accent/[0.06] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>
    <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto section-reveal">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-[#0e0e11] overflow-hidden"
           style="box-shadow:0 32px 80px rgba(0,0,0,0.6),0 0 0 1px rgba(255,255,255,0.025)">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 border-b border-border bg-surface">
          <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-error/70"></span>
          <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-yellow-500/70"></span>
          <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-accent/70"></span>
          <span class="ml-3 text-xs font-mono text-hint">Knot, peer-to-peer version control</span>
        </div>
        <img src="/screenshots/knot/dashboard.png" alt="Knot desktop dashboard, showing protected folders, paired devices, lifetime saves and this week's activity" loading="lazy" class="w-full block" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 01 HOW IT WORKS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="how" class="relative py-24 px-6 bg-surface border-y border-border overflow-hidden">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-0 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[80%] h-72 bg-accent/[0.08] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>
    <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <div class="section-reveal text-center mb-14">
        <SectionEyebrow num="01" label="HOW IT WORKS" />
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-black text-primary mb-4">Pairing is the only fiddly part</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
        {[
          { step: '01', title: 'The folder', body: 'Any folder works. You tell Knot once, and from then on it is a real git repository, because under the hood it always was one.' },
          { step: '02', title: 'The peers',  body: 'The other person installs Knot too. You generate a one-time pairing code, send it however you normally send things, and then both machines show a confirmation that has to be clicked at both ends.' },
          { step: '03', title: 'The save',   body: 'Write code. Hit save. The commit and the push happen without you.' },
        ].map(({ step, title, body }) => (
          <div class="section-reveal relative pl-8 border-l border-border hover:border-accent/40 transition-colors">
            <span class="absolute -left-px top-0 w-0.5 h-8 bg-accent"></span>
            <p class="text-accent font-mono text-xs mb-2">{step}</p>
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-primary mb-2">{title}</h4>
            <p class="text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed">{body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 02 WHAT IT DOES (4 features + who-it's-for chips) ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="features" class="relative py-24 px-6 max-w-6xl mx-auto">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-0 right-0 w-[40%] h-72 bg-accent/[0.06] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>

    <div class="section-reveal text-center mb-14">
      <SectionEyebrow num="02" label="DAY TO DAY" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-black text-primary mb-4">What Knot does once it is running</h2>
      <p class="text-secondary max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        Built and tested on two machines, a MacBook and an HP EliteBook running Ubuntu: first over Wi-Fi, then over Tor between two different networks, which is where it first worked end to end, in May 2026.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      {/* OWNER DECISION: this card used to carry the AI-agents framing. What the repo
           can back is the 2026-08-27 acceptance run: two paired daemons over real onions,
           ~198 s, identical merge oid. An agent on each machine is proven for chat
           (2026-05-31) but not for this merge, so that claim is not made here. */}
      <FeatureBlock
        icon="🤝"
        title="Both machines, the same merge commit"
        description="On 27 August 2026 two paired peers whose histories had diverged merged over Tor four seconds apart, with nothing coordinating them, and both landed on the identical merge commit. About 198 seconds end to end."
      />
      {/* OWNER DECISION: first person "I" proposed here. The strongest version of this
           card is the story: "I once left mine at 170 by accident and spent an afternoon
           wondering why nothing was committing." Needs the owner's yes on person, and on
           the 170. */}
      <FeatureBlock
        icon="💾"
        title="Saves become synced commits"
        description="Ten seconds after your last save in a folder, the change is a real git commit, and it goes out encrypted end to end to every paired machine. Neither end has to open a router port for that."
      />
      <FeatureBlock
        icon="🪢"
        title="Real git underneath"
        description="Knot uses git's real object format, so git log, git diff and git branch all still work, and so does anything else you already point at the folder."
      />
      <FeatureBlock
        icon="🧾"
        title="What a commit carries"
        description="The signature and the agent-session record sit in refs/notes, which does not replicate, so today the full chain can only be produced on the machine that wrote the commits, and moving both inside the commit object is in flight. The authoring device is already there: it rides in the commit itself, so every machine that receives one has it."
      />
    </div>

    <!-- who it's for, compressed from a full section to three chips -->
    <div class="section-reveal flex flex-col sm:flex-row flex-wrap justify-center gap-3 mt-10">
      <span class="px-4 py-2 rounded-full bg-surface border border-border text-xs text-secondary"><span class="text-accent font-mono">SOLO</span> · your laptop and your home server</span>
      <span class="px-4 py-2 rounded-full bg-surface border border-border text-xs text-secondary"><span class="text-accent font-mono">TEAMS</span> · one pairing code per person, no seats to buy</span>
      <span class="px-4 py-2 rounded-full bg-surface border border-border text-xs text-secondary"><span class="text-accent font-mono">ENTERPRISE</span> · code stays inside the network; agents are scoped by path</span>
    </div>

    <p class="section-reveal text-center text-hint text-xs mt-8 max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
      For AI agents, Knot maps a repo's whole shape in one call instead of many: measured ≥3.4× fewer tokens on a 56-file repo the agent had never read, one knot map --tokens 1500 call against five or more files of reading, and you can run the comparison on your own machine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 03 GET IT (moved up: this is what the page is for) ────────────────── -->
  <section id="download" class="relative py-28 px-6 bg-surface border-y border-border overflow-hidden">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-0 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[70%] h-80 bg-accent/[0.10] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>
    <div class="relative max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <div class="section-reveal text-center">
        <SectionEyebrow num="03" label="INSTALL" />
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-black text-primary mb-4">Install it</h2>
        <p class="text-secondary mb-10 leading-relaxed">
          Free, for macOS and Linux. There is no Windows build, because the daemon and the app talk over Unix domain sockets, and nobody has ported that to named pipes. The macOS build is not notarized either, so the first open is blocked; clearing it takes one command, below.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
          <a
            href="https://github.com/pointbreaklab-hub/knot/releases/latest/download/Knot-macos.dmg"
            class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-6 py-4 rounded-xl bg-accent text-bg font-bold text-sm hover:bg-white transition-all hover:scale-105 hover:shadow-[0_0_32px_rgba(0,212,170,0.4)]"
          >
            Download for macOS
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/pointbreaklab-hub/knot/releases/latest/download/knot-vcs-amd64.deb"
            class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-6 py-4 rounded-xl bg-accent text-bg font-bold text-sm hover:bg-white transition-all hover:scale-105 hover:shadow-[0_0_32px_rgba(0,212,170,0.4)]"
          >
            Download for Linux
          </a>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-secondary mb-6">
          The <a href="/knot/docs/" class="text-accent hover:underline">installation guide</a> covers macOS, Linux, verifying your download, and your first synced save.
        </p>

        <!-- The VS Code extension: a client of the same daemon, framed as an
             add-on, not a third platform. Live at PointBreakLab.knot-vcs. -->
        <a
          href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=PointBreakLab.knot-vcs"
          class="flex items-center gap-4 text-left px-6 py-4 rounded-xl bg-bg border border-border hover:border-accent/40 hover:bg-accent/5 transition-all group mb-6"
        >
          <svg width="26" height="26" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" class="text-accent shrink-0">
            <path d="M8.5 7.5 4 12l4.5 4.5M15.5 7.5 20 12l-4.5 4.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
          <span class="flex-1">
            <span class="block text-sm font-bold text-primary group-hover:text-accent transition-colors">Also on the VS Code Marketplace</span>
            <span class="block text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">Same daemon as the app, driven from the editor sidebar instead of a second window.</span>
          </span>
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" class="text-hint group-hover:text-accent transition-colors shrink-0">
            <path d="M3 8h10M9 4l4 4-4 4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
        </a>

        <div class="text-left p-5 rounded-2xl bg-bg border border-border font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">
          <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">Version</span><span class="text-primary">v1.0.2 · build 20260826.2319</span></div>
          <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">macOS</span><span class="text-primary/80">Intel, and Apple Silicon via Rosetta · .dmg</span></div>
          <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">Linux</span><span class="text-primary/80">x86_64 · .deb (Ubuntu 20.04+, Debian 11+)</span></div>
          <div class="flex gap-3 mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-border"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">SHA-256 mac</span><span class="text-primary/60 break-all">8eb0f6d2d57340e379c878b63077a1a4cb37963e9d7d44764e6a7c1deab9aa84</span></div>
          <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">SHA-256 linux</span><span class="text-primary/60 break-all">7e64c9aebca4b57a9e4e9ec0bff2e517ace921681b7da1779aa7c03d6223f938</span></div>
        </div>

        <!-- First-run steps (self-contained, ad-hoc-signed, not notarized). -->
        <details class="section-reveal group text-left mt-4 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-bg border border-border">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center gap-3 text-sm font-bold text-primary">
            <span class="text-accent font-mono transition-transform group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
            First-run steps (macOS &amp; Linux)
          </summary>
          <div class="text-xs space-y-4 mt-4">
            <div>
              <div class="font-mono text-hint uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">macOS</div>
              <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-2">macOS blocks the first open. You clear that once, and any of these does it:</p>
              <ul class="text-secondary/90 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
                <li>Most reliable (any macOS): open Terminal and run<br><code class="text-primary/80 break-all">xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "/Applications/Knot.app"</code></li>
                <li>Or open Knot once (it's blocked), then <span class="text-primary/80">System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → “Open Anyway”</span>.</li>
                <li>Older macOS (pre-Sequoia): right-click Knot → Open → Open.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="pt-3 border-t border-border">
              <div class="font-mono text-hint uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Linux</div>
              <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-2">Tor and the on-device AI runtime ship inside the package. apt handles the dependencies, the systemd unit and upgrades.</p>
              <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed"><code>sudo apt install ./knot-vcs-amd64.deb
systemctl --user enable --now knot-vcs
knot-app           # or `knot solo` in any folder</code></pre>
              <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed mt-2">Current Ubuntu ships no GUI that installs a local .deb file; App Center is snap-only, and that is Ubuntu-wide, every vendor's .deb included. Details in the <a href="/knot/docs/" class="text-accent hover:underline">installation guide</a>.</p>
            </div>
            <p class="text-hint pt-1">Your code never leaves your machines regardless of the signature.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <p class="text-hint text-xs mt-4">
          Full release history: <a href="/knot/changelog/" class="text-accent hover:underline">changelog</a>. Bug reports and use cases both welcome: <a href="mailto:mail.roshankumargupta@gmail.com?subject=Knot" class="text-accent hover:underline">Get in touch</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 04 ARCHITECTURE (one diagram; spec + mesh moved to the white paper) ── -->
  <section id="architecture" class="relative py-28 px-6 max-w-4xl mx-auto overflow-hidden">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-0 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[80%] h-72 bg-accent/[0.06] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>
    <div class="relative">
      <div class="section-reveal text-center mb-12">
        <SectionEyebrow num="04" label="ARCHITECTURE" />
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-light text-primary mb-4 tracking-tight">How a commit gets to the other machine</h2>
        {/* CONFIRM before publishing: the date the alphabetical-base bug was found and
             the size of the bundles it was shipping. */}
        <p class="text-secondary max-w-xl mx-auto">
          For a while every push shipped the whole history again, because the base commit was taken off a list that git sorts by branch name, and genesis always sorted first. The cryptography was the easy part: Ed25519 identities, mutual authentication, a Tor circuit carrying nothing but encrypted bytes, and real git objects at both ends.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-reveal">
        <KnotArchitectureDiagram />
      </div>
      <p class="section-reveal text-hint text-xs text-center mt-6 max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        Whispr's messages travel over the same Tor primitives, and the objects on the wire are the same format git itself writes. None of that cryptography was invented for Knot; it is Tor's, and the Ed25519 that OpenSSH ships.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 05 FAQ (trimmed 7 → 4) ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="faq" class="relative py-24 px-6 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-0 left-1/4 w-[50%] h-72 bg-accent/[0.06] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>

    <div class="section-reveal text-center mb-12">
      <SectionEyebrow num="05" label="FAQ" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-black text-primary mb-4">Safety, servers, git, and the offline case</h2>
    </div>

    {/* OWNER DECISION: first person "I" proposed here. The safety answer currently ends
         "...by anyone outside this project, which is one person in Würzburg." In the first
         person it would read "...by anyone but me. I am one person in Würzburg", which is
         blunter and is the same fact. */}
    <div class="space-y-4">
      {[
        {
          q: 'Is Knot safe?',
          a: 'Every connection uses Ed25519 mutual authentication: both sides cryptographically prove their identity before any byte of code crosses the wire. The wire itself is Tor (.onion hidden services), so bytes are encrypted end-to-end and relays only ever see ciphertext. Pairing a new device needs a confirmation click on both machines. The primitives are the ones Signal, Tor and OpenSSH ship; the code wiring them together has not been reviewed by anyone outside this project, which is one person in Würzburg.',
        },
        {
          q: 'Does Knot use a server?',
          a: 'No. Code transfer is always peer-to-peer, through Tor circuits. There is an optional small "coordinator" that helps peers find each other across the internet, but it never sees a byte of your code, only "peer X hosts project Y." You can run it on a $35 Raspberry Pi, or skip it entirely.',
        },
        {
          q: 'Do I still get to use git?',
          a: 'Yes. Knot uses git\'s real object format, so a Knot folder is also a real git repository. git log, git diff, git branch, git checkout, all work. When two histories diverge Knot merges them for you, and because the merge commit is built deterministically both machines end up on the identical one. If the same lines actually conflict it stops and parks the incoming side, and then you resolve it in plain git like anyone else.',
        },
        {
          q: 'What if my collaborator is offline when I save?',
          a: 'Nothing transfers until they come back online, same as any peer-to-peer protocol. Your commit is safe on your machine; queued commits sync automatically when their daemon reconnects. A third always-on peer bridges the gap: a home server or a $5/month VPS running the same free build.',
        },
      ].map(({ q, a }) => (
        <details class="section-reveal group p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border hover:border-accent/30 transition-colors">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-start gap-4">
            <span class="text-accent text-xl font-mono shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
            <span class="text-base font-bold text-primary leading-snug">{q}</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3 pl-9">
            {a}
          </p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer />
</Base>
